WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.930
I always had this kind of
idea that magic mushrooms

00:00:01.930 --> 00:00:05.010
just grow deep in the forest,
and they're hard to identify.

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:07.100
So I went out to wander around,

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:08.510
and I brought a little paper bag with me

00:00:08.510 --> 00:00:09.810
that, if I saw mushrooms,

00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:11.970
and I did, because they
were like everywhere.

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:13.970
Sean is a Portland-based social worker

00:00:13.970 --> 00:00:16.690
who asked that we only use
his first name in this story.

00:00:16.690 --> 00:00:18.790
In 2016, he and his girlfriend

00:00:18.790 --> 00:00:20.810
were foraging for psychedelic mushrooms

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:22.120
in a nearby state park,

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:24.110
when they were stopped by a park ranger.

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:26.410
They saw me bending down,
and they screeched to a halt

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:28.790
and came up and started questioning me.

00:00:28.790 --> 00:00:29.623
I kinda knew it was illegal,

00:00:29.623 --> 00:00:31.540
but I thought, ah, they'll
give me a ticket or something.

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:32.710
I'm going to get chewed out.

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:34.610
Maybe they'll kick me out
of the park or something.

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:35.680
He was arrested

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:37.670
and spent the weekend in county jail.

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:39.600
He pled guilty to felony possession,

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:40.990
which derailed his career.

00:00:40.990 --> 00:00:42.420
He was fired from his job

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:44.300
at a community mental health agency.

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:46.940
And it has made him ineligible
to receive insurance

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:48.400
in his private practice.

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:49.780
Professionally, it was devastating.

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:51.800
Personally, it was devastating.
It was really hard.

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:54.640
It's still impacting me to this day.

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:55.650
Four years later,

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:58.880
Oregon voters decriminalized
low-level drug possession.

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:01.090
If that law had been in place in 2016,

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:04.930
the park ranger would probably
have ticketed John $100.

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:07.100
In a separate 2020 ballot initiative

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:10.350
Oregon voters also opted
to legalize psilocybin,

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:12.810
the psychoactive compound
and magic mushrooms,

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:14.950
for use in therapeutic settings.

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:17.250
Regulators have until January 2023

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:18.860
to begin issuing licenses.

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:19.693
I think it's a great thing

00:01:19.693 --> 00:01:22.180
to be able to start to
have this system in place,

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:25.500
so that we'll be able to do
that work above ground, legally.

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:26.930
But Sean didn't wait around

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:28.840
for the state licensing program.

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:31.230
He started treating his
clients with psychedelics

00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:32.680
after his felony conviction

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:34.880
destroyed his traditional practice,

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:37.830
under the condition that
they supply the drugs.

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:40.700
Psilocybin from magic
mushrooms has real potential

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:43.040
to revolutionize mental health treatments.

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:45.800
Really effective in ways
that other medications

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:47.440
that are available now are not

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:50.830
to help us in ways that dress
bigger existential questions

00:01:50.830 --> 00:01:53.320
about the meaning of life,
our place in the universe,

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:56.610
and allow the deep work that otherwise

00:01:56.610 --> 00:01:58.160
in therapy can take years.

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:01.390
There's robust evidence
that therapy with MDMA,

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:03.490
or ecstasy, is an effective treatment

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:05.410
for post-traumatic stress disorder.

00:02:05.410 --> 00:02:08.860
And the FDA has deemed
psilocybin a breakthrough therapy

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:10.520
for treating severe depression.

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:13.410
Sean says that psilocybin has
helped many of his clients

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:14.790
overcome traumatic episodes,

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:17.380
including a middle-aged
man whose life was upended

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:19.460
after he was robbed at gunpoint.

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:21.610
No other treatment options had worked.

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:23.700
He was desperate for some
relief when he came to me,

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:27.030
and we did just one
session with mushrooms,

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:30.880
and an hour into it or so, I
asked him how he was doing,

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:34.880
and he said, I opened this
door to this vast space.

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:37.070
Having this kind of vision
then of himself as a child

00:02:37.070 --> 00:02:38.960
in this vast open field,

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:42.250
just being able to run
free and feel that joy.

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:45.320
And after a while of not
being able to find that fear

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:48.810
that had been this
ever-present thing in his life,

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:52.040
this thing had been
haunting him for years,

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:54.490
he considered a complete cure.

00:02:54.490 --> 00:02:56.310
Researchers have resumed studying

00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:58.760
the use of psychedelics
in therapeutic settings,

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:02.450
picking on promising research
that began nearly 80 years ago

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:04.360
but that was halted by drug prohibition.

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:07.230
When psychedelics were first discovered

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:09.450
in the early 40s and
then really distributed

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:13.060
to the psychiatrists around
the world in the 50s,

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:15.260
it quickly became apparent

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:17.250
that psychedelics had the potential

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:19.550
to become the cutting edge
of psychiatric research.

00:03:19.550 --> 00:03:22.010
So in a sense, the
mystical mimetic properties

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:25.130
of these drugs were a highly unusual.

00:03:25.130 --> 00:03:28.090
Harbor-UCLA Medical Center
psychiatrist Charles Grob

00:03:28.090 --> 00:03:29.620
published a seminal study,

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:32.580
which found that psilocybin
demonstrably alleviates

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:35.570
the end-of-life anxiety of
terminal cancer patients.

00:03:35.570 --> 00:03:38.420
What we observed with this
study was that our subjects

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:41.610
to begin with were in
great existential crisis.

00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:43.920
Their sense of self had eroded,

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:46.020
the sense of continuity over time,

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:49.150
often a sense of loss of
meaning, loss of purpose.

00:03:49.150 --> 00:03:52.110
What we found with these
psychedelic experiences,

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:54.550
individuals reported they were often able

00:03:54.550 --> 00:03:57.830
to reestablish that
sense of self continuity

00:03:57.830 --> 00:03:59.750
with the previous parts of their lives

00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:02.770
and strengthen their sense
of meaning and purpose.

00:04:02.770 --> 00:04:06.470
Denver decriminalized psilocybin in 2019.

00:04:06.470 --> 00:04:09.530
Oakland decriminalized all
naturally occurring psychedelics

00:04:09.530 --> 00:04:13.140
later that year, and Santa
Cruz followed in early 2020.

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:15.760
The California legislature
is considering legalizing

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:17.760
several psychedelics statewide,

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:19.400
but Oregon is the first state

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:21.200
that will establish a legal framework

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:23.350
for the sales and use of psychedelics

00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:24.730
in a therapeutic setting.

00:04:24.730 --> 00:04:27.270
I don't believe that the state
should have anything to do

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:29.470
with what's going on in my mind.

00:04:29.470 --> 00:04:32.570
I never signed off, nor
do I know anybody else

00:04:32.570 --> 00:04:34.007
who signed off and said,

00:04:34.007 --> 00:04:37.030
yeah, you get to control
what I put in my brain.

00:04:37.030 --> 00:04:38.890
Joe Eaton is a retired counselor

00:04:38.890 --> 00:04:40.670
who now works as a psychedelic sitter,

00:04:40.670 --> 00:04:42.820
taking care of his clients
while they're tripping.

00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:45.290
He may not be eligible
for a state license.

00:04:45.290 --> 00:04:48.160
I come to the substances
with the idea that,

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:50.870
whatever you want to do with them is fine.

00:04:50.870 --> 00:04:52.870
You just want to party? That's fine.

00:04:52.870 --> 00:04:55.690
And you want to hire me to
keep you safe? That's fine.

00:04:55.690 --> 00:04:58.710
You want to seriously do a search for God?

00:04:58.710 --> 00:05:00.060
Great. Let's do that.

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:05.060
You want to do psychodynamic
work on your trauma issues?

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:07.110
Sure. Let's do that.

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:09.340
Some psychiatrists, including Grob,

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:12.510
are concerned that, if
psychedelic use is unregulated,

00:05:12.510 --> 00:05:14.630
it could put some patients in danger,

00:05:14.630 --> 00:05:18.140
such as people with schizophrenia
or bipolar disorder.

00:05:18.140 --> 00:05:19.341
The experience,

00:05:19.341 --> 00:05:21.880
particularly under adverse conditions,

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:25.100
may push them over the edge
into a psychotic state.

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:27.260
Eaton says that a regulatory apparatus

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:29.630
isn't necessary to
protect this population.

00:05:29.630 --> 00:05:32.710
I don't think keeping it in
the hands of the professionals

00:05:32.710 --> 00:05:35.870
who have clearly shown that
they're gonna screw it up,

00:05:35.870 --> 00:05:37.280
they've shown they're gonna screw it up

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:38.430
time and time again

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:43.240
by listening to Nixon and his
cohorts for the last 50 years.

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:44.740
I just don't have a lot of respect

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:47.520
for any of the professional organizations.

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:48.820
Oregon's law created

00:05:48.820 --> 00:05:51.340
the Oregon Psilocybin Advisory Board

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:52.650
to work with the health department

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:54.860
to create licenses and training programs

00:05:54.860 --> 00:05:57.990
for manufacturing, selling
and testing psilocybin,

00:05:57.990 --> 00:06:02.040
as well as providing broadly
defined psilocybin services.

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:05.200
I can at least imagine certain
kind of training programs

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:08.100
and what sorts of things I
think would be good to have

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:09.120
in something like that.

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:11.530
But I don't think that
somebody needs to have gone

00:06:11.530 --> 00:06:13.610
to a Master's program in social work,

00:06:13.610 --> 00:06:15.310
MD program in psychiatry,

00:06:15.310 --> 00:06:19.860
to be able to safely and
effectively dispense and guide

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:21.580
the use of these substances

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:23.490
to facilitate their therapeutic use.

00:06:23.490 --> 00:06:26.160
The good news is that
practitioners like Eaton

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:28.390
who may not be obtaining
an official license

00:06:28.390 --> 00:06:31.580
will still be mostly protected,
thanks to Oregon's decision

00:06:31.580 --> 00:06:34.050
to decriminalize
low-level drug possession.

00:06:34.050 --> 00:06:37.290
Sean plans to apply for a
license as soon as he can.

00:06:37.290 --> 00:06:39.220
Very often, when people have bad trips

00:06:39.220 --> 00:06:40.360
in more recreational contexts,

00:06:40.360 --> 00:06:41.520
it's because they get some idea

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:42.580
that they're gonna get busted,

00:06:42.580 --> 00:06:43.700
they're going to get in trouble legally,

00:06:43.700 --> 00:06:46.360
and that anxiety then spirals.

00:06:46.360 --> 00:06:49.850
So I do think that just having
that legal safety is huge.

00:06:49.850 --> 00:06:53.500
So I already had pretty strong
opinions about the drug war,

00:06:53.500 --> 00:06:56.700
and certainly having
that personal experience,

00:06:56.700 --> 00:06:58.640
I guess maybe care about it that much more

00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:02.030
and realize how severely
it can impact person's life

00:07:02.030 --> 00:07:06.947
to get tangled up in the criminal
justice system like that.

